The X reads are ported from version 1.8.7 (npm package @jackwener/opencli@1.8.7), the version Agent Reach
1.6.1 installs and whose X commands were tested live on 2026-11-09.

Files in page_scripts/ (page scripts sent to the browser):
- dom_stable.js, auto_scroll.js: src/browser/dom-helpers.ts
- linkedin_auth_probe.js: clis/linkedin/shared.js (buildAuthProbeScript)
- instagram_read.js: field mapping from clis/instagram/profile.js and user.js
- x_read.js (1.8.7): clis/twitter/profile.js, user-timeline.js, tweets.js, search.js, thread.js, shared.js and
  utils.js (web app token, query ids and feature flags, request headers, query-id lookup in X's scripts,
  profile and tweet mapping, timeline paging)

Python ports:
- linkedin/page.py: clis/linkedin/shared.js (login-wall check, whitespace clean-up)
- instagram/page.py: clis/instagram/_shared/read-response.js (error mapping)
- x/page.py (1.8.7): clis/twitter/shared.js (describeTwitterApiError: error mapping)

Changes made by Tool Store:
- Each script is cut out of its OpenCLI source file and saved on its own, so Python can send it to the page.
- dom_stable.js and auto_scroll.js take their numbers as a function argument instead of text templating.
- The command logic around the scripts is rewritten in Python for per-user browser sessions.
- instagram_read.js no longer calls Instagram's web API, which Instagram's website stopped using (it answers
  404); it reads the profile and its first 12 posts from the JSON data the profile page already carries, falls
  back to the page summary for counts, returns booleans instead of Yes/No, keeps full captions, adds each
  post's link and date, and reports logged-out and "please wait" pages as their own errors.
- x_read.js holds the four reads Agent Reach offers (search, profile, tweets, thread) in one script. It reads
  the ct0 cookie from the page instead of the browser's cookie store, looks up a renamed query id in X's own
  website scripts only after X refuses the built-in one (the GitHub list of query ids is not used), finds
  tweets and cursors with one shared timeline walk, picks the highest-bitrate video, gives dates in ISO 8601,
  asks for view counts in threads too, and leaves out link cards, author bios and engagement re-ranking.

